  "id": "Compound::DB00284 - Gene::8972",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which Acarbose may interact with Mgam, through the inhibition of amylase activity. Acarbose, a glucose analog, binds to the active site of amyloinvertase, an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of starch and glycogen. This binding prevents the enzyme from catalyzing the breakdown of carbohydrates, thereby reducing glucose absorption in the small intestine. The evidence from multiple paths (path_8402, path_8403, path_8404) consistently shows that Acarbose binds to Amy1a and Amy1b, which are isoforms of the same enzyme, and that both bind to the same pathway—Amylase Activity, which is involved in carbohydrate digestion. The shared participation in Digestion Of Dietary Carbohydrate by Amy1a and Amy1b, and the subsequent participation in Mgam, indicates a coordinated role in carbohydrate metabolism. The high scores in all three paths suggest a strong, biologically plausible link between Acarbose and Mgam, likely through the inhibition of amylase activity. The mechanism is consistent across different isoforms, reinforcing the likelihood of a general effect on Mgam.",
